<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · Love song · 1976 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sweet Mother</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A happy song from 1976 that thanks mothers, sung in Nigerian Pidgin English. It became one of the best-loved songs in Africa.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Prince Nico Mbarga</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Prince Nico Mbarga</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Nigerian and Cameroonian highlife</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nigerian Pidgin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Onitsha, Nigeria (where it was released)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>demo turned down in 1974; released December 1976</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (a song by Prince Nico Mbarga and Rocafil Jazz, released 1976)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Sweet Mother&quot; is a highlife song by the Cameroonian and Nigerian singer Prince Nico Mbarga and his band Rocafil Jazz. Its demo tape was turned down by EMI in 1974, which cited the song's &quot;childish appeal&quot;, and it was later rejected by Decca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philips">Philips</a> Records too. It was finally released in December 1976 by Rogers All Stars, a Nigerian recording company based in Onitsha. The song is sung in Nigerian Pidgin English; its music is West African highlife, with guitar finger-picking in the style of Congolese soukous.</p><p>&quot;Sweet Mother&quot; became one of the most popular hits in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, selling between 3 and 13 million copies, and it is sometimes called &quot;Africa's anthem&quot;. In 2004 the readers and listeners of the BBC voted it Africa's favourite song, ahead of Brenda Fassie's &quot;Vuli Ndlela&quot;, Fela Kuti's &quot;Lady&quot;, Franco's &quot;Mario&quot; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba">Miriam Makeba</a>'s version of &quot;Malaika&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A celebration of motherhood: a song of love and thanks to one's mother, warm and joyful in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="4434889" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Prince-nico-mbarga-and-rocafil-jazz-sweet-mother-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
